codemap 8
path claude-hooks
kind flow
author ai
group tooling
note The Claude Code hooks: cargo xtask hook <event>, run by Claude Code around the agent's tool calls and turns. They are the process half of the rulebook: archlint and the gate say what code is acceptable, the hooks make sure an agent cannot get around them. Before a tool runs, edits to the rulebook, the map files and the goldens are denied, each with the one way that file is written (the owner, codemap commands, the tests), and a commit is refused unless the gate is green for the current working copy. After an edit to a .rs file, rustfmt formats it and archlint checks it, and the findings go straight back to the agent, so it fixes a rule the moment it breaks it. When the agent stops, the gate runs and a red gate blocks the stop with its failures; after three identical red runs the session may end, the gate stays red, commits stay refused, and the next session starts with the failures in its context. Hooks are Rust in the same binary rather than shell scripts, so they run wherever the workspace builds. The shell-command guard is a best effort against a writing command naming a guarded path; the gate's rulebook check is the back-stop.

